Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream iss(markdown);

    while (iss >> line) {
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Title\n\nThis is a first paragraph.\nThis is a second paragraph.\nThis is a third paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and then wraps each line in a `<p>` tag to create the HTML. The `main` function demonstrates how to use this function by converting a sample markdown string to HTML and printing the result.

Note: This function assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and does not contain any errors or inconsistencies.

